# Solution — GIAM 4.2 Problem 1

## Problem

Insert either $ \in $ or $ \subseteq $ in the blanks in the following sentences (in order to produce true sentences).

$$$
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{i)} \;\; 1 \;\underline{\qquad}\; \bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} &\qquad
\text{iii)} \;\; \{a,b\} \;\underline{\qquad}\; \bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} \\[4pt]
\text{ii)} \;\; \{a\} \;\underline{\qquad}\; \bigl\{a,\{a,b\}\bigr\} &\qquad
\text{iv)} \;\; \bigl\{\{a,b\}\bigr\} \;\underline{\qquad}\; \bigl\{a,\{a,b\}\bigr\}
\end{array}
$$$

## Answer

$$$
\boxed{\;\text{i) } \in \qquad \text{ii) } \subseteq \qquad \text{iii) } \in \qquad \text{iv) } \subseteq\;}
$$$

## The Two Tests

Everything turns on a single distinction:

$$$
\begin{array}{l|l}
L \in R & \text{is } L \text{ itself one of the things listed inside } R \,? \\
L \subseteq R & \text{is every } \textit{element of } L \text{ one of the things listed inside } R \,?
\end{array}
$$$

The first treats $ L $ as a single object; the second opens $ L $ up and looks at its contents. Note also that $ L\subseteq R $ only makes sense when $ L $ is a set.

## Case by Case

> **i) $ 1 \in \bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $.** The right-hand side lists four things: $ 3 $, $ 2 $, $ 1 $ and $ \{a,b\} $. The number $ 1 $ is the third of them, so $ \in $ holds. Note $ \subseteq $ could not apply here in any case — $ 1 $ is not a set, so it has no elements to compare.
>
> **ii) $ \{a\} \subseteq \bigl\{a,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $.** The right-hand side lists two things: $ a $ and $ \{a,b\} $. The set $ \{a\} $ is *not* one of them, so $ \in $ fails. But the only element of $ \{a\} $ is $ a $, which **is** listed — so every element of the left side is in the right side, and $ \subseteq $ holds.
>
> **iii) $ \{a,b\} \in \bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $.** Here $ \{a,b\} $ appears literally as the fourth item in the list, so $ \in $ holds. And $ \subseteq $ fails: its elements $ a $ and $ b $ are *not* among $ 3,2,1,\{a,b\} $.
>
> **iv) $ \bigl\{\{a,b\}\bigr\} \subseteq \bigl\{a,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $.** The left side is a set with one element, namely $ \{a,b\} $. That element is listed on the right, so $ \subseteq $ holds. But $ \bigl\{\{a,b\}\bigr\} $ itself is not one of the two items listed, so $ \in $ fails.

All eight membership and subset tests were evaluated directly, confirming that in each of the four blanks **exactly one** of the two symbols works.

## The Picture

!(84%)[The four cases with both tests run on each](problem-1-inclusion.svg "A diagram in four parts. The first states the two tests: L is an element of R asks whether L itself is one of the things listed inside R; L is a subset of R asks whether every element of L is one of the things listed inside R. The first treats L as a single object, the second opens L up and asks about its contents. The centre shows four cards, one per blank. Case i, in green: 1 is an element of the set 3, 2, 1, and the set a b — because 1 is one of the four things listed, and subset cannot even apply since 1 is not a set. Case iii, also green: the set a b is an element of the set 3, 2, 1, and the set a b — because it is listed as the fourth element; not a subset, since a and b are not themselves listed. Case ii, in red: the set containing a is a subset of the set containing a and the set a b — because its only element a is in the right side; not an element, since the elements listed are a and the set a b, not the set containing a. Case iv, also red: the set containing the set a b is a subset of the set containing a and the set a b — because its only element, the set a b, is in the right side; not an element, since the set containing the set a b is not itself listed. A table then runs both tests on all four cases, showing element yes and subset no for cases i and iii, and element no and subset yes for cases ii and iv, so exactly one passes each time. A final band describes the pattern in the four answers: comparing cases iii and iv, the right sides differ but so does the number of braces on the left. The set a b is an element of the set 3, 2, 1, and the set a b; while the set containing the set a b is a subset of the set containing a and the set a b. Wrapping an object in one more pair of braces turns a membership statement into a subset statement, because the extra layer is exactly what subset strips off before comparing. All eight membership and subset tests were evaluated directly.")

## Remark — One Extra Pair of Braces Flips the Symbol

Parts (iii) and (iv) are the heart of the exercise. Strip away the different right-hand sides and look at what changed on the left:

$$$
\{a,b\} \;\in\; R \qquad\text{versus}\qquad \bigl\{\{a,b\}\bigr\} \;\subseteq\; R' .
$$$

Adding one pair of braces converts an $ \in $ statement into a $ \subseteq $ statement. That is not a coincidence — it is a general fact:

$$$
x \in R \qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad \{x\} \subseteq R .
$$$

The reason is immediate from the definitions. Saying $ \{x\}\subseteq R $ means every element of $ \{x\} $ lies in $ R $, and $ \{x\} $ has exactly one element, namely $ x $. So the two statements say the same thing in different packaging.

This is the cleanest way to keep the symbols straight: **$ \subseteq $ takes one layer of braces off the left-hand side before comparing, and $ \in $ does not.**

## Remark — Why $ \subseteq $ Fails in (iii), and $ \in $ in (ii)

The two failures are instructive in opposite ways.

In **(iii)**, $ \{a,b\}\subseteq\bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $ is false because the test asks about $ a $ and $ b $ individually, and neither is listed on the right. The right-hand side contains the *box* $ \{a,b\} $ but not its *contents*. This is exactly the failure of transitivity noted in 4.1 Problem 3:

$$$
a \in \{a,b\} \in R \qquad\text{does \textbf{not} give}\qquad a \in R .
$$$

In **(ii)**, $ \{a\}\in\bigl\{a,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $ is false for the mirror-image reason: the right side contains $ a $ but not the box $ \{a\} $. One might paraphrase: (iii) has the box but wants the contents, (ii) has the contents but wants the box.

## Remark — Both Symbols Can Hold at Once, Though Not Here

In all four blanks exactly one symbol works, which makes the exercise clean. That is not forced in general — there are sets for which **both** relations hold:

$$$
\bigl\{\{a\}\bigr\} \;\in\; \Bigl\{\{a\},\ \bigl\{\{a\}\bigr\}\Bigr\} \qquad\text{and}\qquad \bigl\{\{a\}\bigr\} \;\subseteq\; \Bigl\{\{a\},\ \bigl\{\{a\}\bigr\}\Bigr\} ,
$$$

since the right side lists $ \bigl\{\{a\}\bigr\} $ (giving $ \in $) and also lists $ \{a\} $, the sole element of the left side (giving $ \subseteq $).

The simplest example of the phenomenon is $ \varnothing $ and $ \{\varnothing\} $, from 4.1 Problem 1:

$$$
\varnothing \in \{\varnothing\} \qquad\text{and}\qquad \varnothing\subseteq\{\varnothing\} ,
$$$

both true. So the exercise's neat one-symbol-each pattern is a property of the particular sets chosen, not a law. It is worth checking both tests rather than stopping at the first that works.

## Remark — A Checklist for Problems of This Kind

The reliable procedure is mechanical.

1. **List the elements of the right-hand side explicitly.** For $ \bigl\{3,2,1,\{a,b\}\bigr\} $ they are $ 3 $, $ 2 $, $ 1 $, $ \{a,b\} $ — four things, one of which happens to be a set.
2. **Ask whether the whole left-hand side appears in that list.** If yes, $ \in $.
3. **If the left-hand side is a set, list its elements and ask whether each appears.** If yes, $ \subseteq $.
4. **If the left-hand side is not a set, $ \subseteq $ is not even a candidate** — as in part (i).

Steps 2 and 3 are different questions, and running both is the only way to be sure. The single most common error at this stage is to run step 3 when the problem wants step 2, which is precisely what part (iii) is testing.
